.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_ALLOCATED_BYTES 3 "2026-09-16" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes \- report the memory a ranged pool holds for its entries
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes(ccol_r_mempool *rmp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the sizes of the entry blocks of all the tiers added together,
in bytes, which is what a ranged pool really costs.

Each tier is a pool of its own with its own element size and element
count, and every tier that is large enough to keep a cache for each
thread holds its own reserve, so this total is larger than the sum of
the tier capacities. Use this figure to fit a ranged pool into a memory
budget, which is the case where working it out by hand is hardest.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp                       Ranged memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the size in bytes of the entry block of every tier, added
together
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Only the entry blocks of the tiers are counted, on the same terms as
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3). The handles, each thread's cache and
every entry that a fallback policy serves are all left out.

The value stays the same for the whole lifetime of the pool.
The function asserts if rmp is NULL.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the lock of each tier in turn, so it belongs on a cold
path; don't use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create (3)
